            <ArticleTitle>Atypical Presentation of Acute Freiberg Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>385-389</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1067-2516(16)30382-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1053/j.jfas.2016.11.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Freiberg disease is a chronic progressive condition that results in pain and loss of normal function of the metatarsophalangeal joint (MTPJ). We describe a case of acute Freiberg disease secondary to a short course of oral steroids. The patient presented with an acute metatarsal head fracture that was managed successfully with open reduction and internal fixation. Although a rare complication of corticosteroid use, physicians having patients start taking steroids must remember the risk of osteonecrosis.</AbstractText>
